\b Cognitive Recognition Framework\b0\par
\b Comprehensive Technical Project Report\b0\par
\par
\b Report Version\b0: 2.0 (Detailed)\par
\b Date\b0: 2026-07-08\par
\b Workspace\b0: Cognitive Recognition framework\par
\par
\b 1. Executive Overview\b0\par
The Cognitive Recognition Framework is a hospital-focused computer vision platform designed for operational safety monitoring, object understanding, and anomaly detection in clinical environments. The system combines multi-model object detection, temporal identity tracking, RGB-D depth reasoning, and rule-based safety analytics in one integrated architecture.\par
\par
At a high level, the framework answers four mission-critical questions:\par
1. What objects are present in the scene now?\par
2. Are those detections stable over time and tied to persistent identities?\par
3. Are the detected objects physically plausible in 3D space?\par
4. Do the observed objects indicate safety anomalies (for example blocked exits, obstructed door zones, or hazardous spillage)?\par
\par
The project goes beyond raw detection by adding context gating, depth-based geometric validation, and temporal memory. This improves reliability for hospital operations where false alarms and missed hazards have direct safety impact.\par
\par
\b 2. Business and Operational Intent\b0\par
Primary intent is to support hospital safety and operational awareness across corridors, wards, treatment spaces, and transitional zones.\par
\par
Target outcomes:\par
- Detect important clinical and infrastructure objects reliably.\par
- Maintain temporal continuity for moving and static assets.\par
- Reduce false positives using real-world physical constraints.\par
- Provide actionable and auditable anomaly alerts, especially for exit safety and obstruction monitoring.\par
- Produce logs and visual evidence suitable for engineering validation and stakeholder reporting.\par
\par
\b 3. Scope of the Repository\b0\par
The workspace is structured to separate code, models, data, outputs, and documentation:\par
- 01_codebase: training, inference, RGB-D, anomaly detection, and object filtering logic.\par
- 02_datasets: datasets and testing media.\par
