\par
\b 4. System Architecture\b0\par
\b 4.1 Logical Layers\b0\par
The implementation follows a layered strategy:\par
Layer A: Data preparation and class harmonization.\par
Layer B: Base object detection (YOLO models).\par
Layer C: Selective open-vocabulary recovery (Grounding DINO).\par
Layer D: Temporal identity persistence (ByteTrack and room-state memory).\par
Layer E: RGB-D spatial reconstruction and 3D consistency checks.\par
Layer F: Rule-based anomaly and safety analytics.\par
Layer G: Reporting and persistence (Excel, CSV, annotated media, SQLite).\par
\par
\b 4.2 Why Layered Instead of Single-Model\b0\par
A single detector cannot simultaneously maximize speed, recall on weak classes, temporal stability, and physical plausibility. The architecture intentionally distributes responsibilities:\par
- YOLO handles high-throughput core detection.\par
- DINO recovers weak or missed classes with contextual safeguards.\par
- ByteTrack and hold logic stabilize object continuity and IDs.\par
- RGB-D plus geometric filters enforce real-world constraints.\par
- Rule engines convert detections into safety outcomes.\par
\par
\b 5. Data Preparation and Curation Pipeline\b0\par
Data preparation scripts are responsible for class quality, split integrity, and training balance.\par
\par
\b 5.1 Class Filtering and Remapping\b0\par
The preparation flow applies minimum class-support thresholds for train and validation sets. Classes with insufficient representation are removed, surviving classes are remapped to a compact contiguous index range, and labels are rewritten accordingly.\par
\par
Benefits:\par
- Removes ultra-sparse classes that destabilize optimization.\par
- Keeps class IDs coherent for downstream training and inference.\par
- Improves precision of reported metrics by reducing noisy tails.\par
\par
\b 5.2 Image Retention Rules\b0\par
If an image loses all labels after filtering, the image is dropped from the curated dataset. This prevents unlabeled noise from entering training and distorting negative sampling behavior.\par
\par
\b 5.3 Oversampling Strategy\b0\par
Minority classes are boosted using controlled duplication with constraints (copy caps and deterministic seed behavior). In V3 extension workflows, class-specific oversampling priorities are used for low-frequency safety classes such as spillage and exit_sign.\par
\par
\b 5.4 Dataset Split and Versioning\b0\par
Additional scripts split newly imported datasets into train and validation partitions and generate aligned YAML descriptors. These scripts preserve repeatability through fixed seeds and standardized folder conventions.\par
\par
\b 6. Model Strategy and Class Evolution\b0\par
\b 6.1 Baseline Model Expansion\b0\par
The training lineage expands from a merged detector space built from COCO and hospital classes to a larger 109-class taxonomy.\par
\par
Important class milestones:\par
- 106-class model: 80 COCO + 26 hospital classes.\par
- 109-class model: adds bag, exit_sign, spillage.\par
\par
\b 6.2 Two-Phase Freeze Training\b0\par
Training scripts implement two-phase adaptation:\par
Phase 1: stronger layer freeze to adapt the output head safely.\par
Phase 2: partial unfreeze with lower learning rate for refined convergence.\par
\par
Why this works:\par
- Protects pretrained low-level features early.\par
- Reduces catastrophic drift during class expansion.\par
- Improves convergence stability for newly added classes.\par
\par
\b 6.3 Class Mapping Governance\b0\par
Class remapping between source datasets and merged IDs is explicitly encoded in scripts, reducing hidden assumptions and enabling reproducible lineage audits.\par
\par
\b 7. Core Inference System\b0\par
\b 7.1 Inference Modes\b0\par
The codebase supports multiple inference entry points:\par
- Single-model V3 inference for quick validation.\par
- V1 plus V3 ensemble inference for broader coverage.\par
- Interactive image and video inference with batch and URL workflows.\par
- Temporal and RGB-D variants for sequence processing.\par
\par
\b 7.2 Ensemble Routing Logic\b0\par
The ensemble path uses class-aware routing:\par
- New classes unique to V3 are sourced from V3.\par
- Overlap classes are merged across V1 and V3 via NMS.\par
- Remaining classes are inherited from the designated base model route.\par
\par
This avoids naive averaging and preserves class ownership semantics.\par
\par
\b 7.3 Confidence and IoU Controls\b0\par
Inference scripts use consistent confidence and IoU thresholds, then add class-specific post-filters for known confusion cases. The approach is intentionally pragmatic: use global defaults plus targeted corrections where model behavior in clinical scenes is known.\par
\par
\b 8. Grounding DINO Fallback Design\b0\par
\b 8.1 Purpose\b0\par
DINO is used as selective recall recovery, not as a full replacement for YOLO. It is activated where YOLO confidence is weak, classes are rare, or domain shift is severe in real hospital-like scenes.\par
\par
\b 8.2 Precision Guardrails\b0\par
DINO usage is tightly controlled through:\par
- per-class prompts and thresholds,\par
- isolated querying for highly confusable classes,\par
- SAHI slicing for tiny object visibility improvement,\par
- context-anchor gates requiring supporting scene evidence,\par
- maximum box-area constraints to suppress scene-scale false regions.\par
\par
\b 8.3 Result\b0\par
This design balances recall improvement and false-positive control, especially for critical classes like surgical_scissor, iv_stand, exit_sign, and select PPE/infrastructure targets.\par
\par
\b 9. Temporal Tracking and Identity Persistence\b0\par
\b 9.1 ByteTrack Integration\b0\par
Temporal scripts apply ByteTrack over per-frame detections to assign persistent track IDs. This transforms frame-local detections into trajectory-level entities.\par
\par
\b 9.2 Occlusion Handling\b0\par
Lost-track buffering allows IDs to persist across short occlusions. If an object reappears within the configured horizon, the same ID is retained. This reduces identity churn and improves operational interpretability.\par
\par
\b 9.3 Room-State Memory\b0\par
Additional room-state hold logic preserves recent object states for a stale-frame window. This smooths temporary detector dropouts and supports robust alert logic (for example PPE or obstruction conditions) without flicker.\par
\par
\b 9.4 Temporal Alert Features\b0\par
Temporal variants include egress-aware logic:\par
- Exit ROI occupancy duration thresholds.\par
- Door and exit_sign geometric consistency checks.\par
- Optional synthetic door generation from exit_sign geometry if door detection drops.\par
\par
\b 10. RGB-D Spatial Twin and 3D Reasoning\b0\par
\b 10.1 Sequence Replay and Synchronization\b0\par
RGB-D scripts replay TUM-style datasets by pairing nearest RGB and depth timestamps under a configurable maximum time difference.\par
\par
\b 10.2 Camera Model and Back-Projection\b0\par
Using camera intrinsics, 2D detections are back-projected into metric 3D coordinates. This enables distance estimation, spatial memory logging, and physically grounded checks.\par
\par
\b 10.3 Spatial Database Integration\b0\par
A SQLite spatial_memory table stores timestamped object class, tracker identity, and XYZ coordinates. This acts as a lightweight digital twin backbone for later querying and analysis.\par
\par
\b 10.4 Hardware and Sensor Paths\b0\par
The ecosystem supports multiple RGB-D paths (TUM replays, RealSense/OpenNI/Orbbec variants), with dedicated scripts and environment configurations for strict GPU execution where needed.\par
\par
\b 11. Object Plausibility and 3D Filtering\b0\par
\b 11.1 Common-Sense Rule Filter\b0\par
A rule module applies quick scene sanity checks to remove physically absurd detections (for example implausible location, extreme aspect ratios, or floor/ceiling contradictions for certain classes).\par
\par
\b 11.2 Dimension-Driven 3D Validation\b0\par
A YAML reference file contains approximate hospital object sizes in meters with typical and acceptable ranges. The 3D filter module uses these limits to validate detection plausibility after depth back-projection.\par
\par
Notable behavior:\par
- Bin size gating is active through small_bin and large_bin constraints.\par
- Spillage can be rejected when estimated floor clearance exceeds configured threshold (0.10 m).\par
\par
\b 11.3 Geometric Method\b0\par
The filter performs orientation-robust dimension estimation using PCA over back-projected points, then compares estimated width and height to class-specific thresholds. This is more robust than axis-aligned pixel heuristics.\par
\par
\b 12. Anomaly Detection Subsystem\b0\par
\b 12.1 Blocked Exit and Door-Zone Logic\b0\par
The blocked-exit branch fuses YOLO, DINO, SAM segmentation, RGB-D geometry, and temporal hold cadence to identify persistent obstructions in keep-clear zones around doors.\par
\par
Core process:\par
1. Detect and segment door region.\par
2. Construct 3D keep-clear geometry (half-cylinder style safety zone).\par
3. Detect non-door objects and refine masks.\par
4. Back-project object mask points to 3D.\par
5. Flag blockage if object points intersect zone over sustained duration.\par
\par
\b 12.2 Robust Bag Reading\b0\par
Reader logic for rosbag2 sqlite streams includes corruption-tolerant behavior, skipping broken zstd/deserialization rows rather than aborting the entire stream. This is important for recovered datasets with partial damage.\par
\par
\b 12.3 Door Distance and Safety Output\b0\par
Distance-focused scripts and obstruction outputs are persisted to AD_Rules_Outputs as timestamped video and CSV artifacts for review and traceability.\par
\par
\b 13. Persistence, Logs, and Auditability\b0\par
The framework emits multiple evidence channels:\par
- Annotated image and video artifacts for visual inspection.\par
- CSV logs for sequence-level numerical analysis.\par
- Excel logs with TP/FP/FN/TN style QA summaries.\par
- SQLite records for temporal-spatial retrieval and post-hoc analytics.\par
\par
This multi-format persistence model supports both ML debugging and operational compliance workflows.\par
\par
